WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.513
- Hello, good afternoon.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.400
Thank you so much for
joining us this afternoon,

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:30.400
and welcome to the Spring
2016 Chubbs Fellowship Lecture

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.150
with our esteemed guest,

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:34.493
the Honorable Norman Y. Mineta.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:36.880
For those of you who don't know me,

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.760
my name is Mary Lui.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:39.730
I'm a professor

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:42.790
in the departments of
American Studies and History,

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:45.860
and the current head of
Timothy Dwight College.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.450
As the Master of the college,

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:49.980
I have the honor of
serving as the custodian

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:52.350
of the illustrious Chubb Fellowship,

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.031
established in 1941,

00:02:54.031 --> 00:02:58.020
out of a prior large donation
for education purposes,

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.170
made in 1936 by Hendon Chubb,

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.030
from the class of 1895.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.340
Since establishment in 1941,

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.860
the Chubb Fund has been used

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:10.150
for the encouragement and aid of students

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:14.000
interested in government
and American public affairs.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.041
The fellowship initially aimed to foster

00:03:16.041 --> 00:03:18.290
an interest in public service,

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:19.870
in local and State affairs,

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:21.620
and then grew over the years

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.990
to include numerous distinguished visitors

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:26.550
in national and international affairs,

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:30.140
as well as leaders in the world
of the arts and humanities,

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:34.470
from Wynton Marsalis, Nobel
laureate Aung San Suu Kyi,

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:37.170
and together, these
incredible Chubb leaders

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:40.180
have inspired generations of Yale students

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.830
to undertake public services
and pursue leadership roles

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.360
in the hopes of creating
a better world today,

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:47.693
and for posterity.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.760
Now with this quick description

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.440
of the history and aims
of the Chubb Fellowship,

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.750
I'm now delighted to introduce
our current Chubb Fellow,

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.550
the Honorable Norman Y. Mineta.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.260
His long and illustrious career

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.810
embodies all of the original
aims of the Chubb Fellowship,

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:08.500
to recognize the importance
of local and State leadership,

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.330
and also exemplifies the ways
in which the Chubb Fellowship,

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.090
in these last few decades,

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:15.682
has expanded to bring in

00:04:15.682 --> 00:04:18.950
important national and world leaders,

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:22.380
to inspire our students on this campus.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.520
His career, spanning
50 years, and counting,

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.730
'cause it's not over yet,
(audience laughing)

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:31.010
certainly hits all of these notes,

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.000
and many, many more.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.720
Norman Yoshio Mineta was
born in San Jose, California,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.880
on November 12th, 1931.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:41.830
Following the bombing of Pearl Harbor

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:44.077
on December 7th, 1941,

00:04:44.077 --> 00:04:47.380
and the signing of Executive Order 9066

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:49.878
on February 19th, 1942,

00:04:49.878 --> 00:04:54.878
just exactly 73 years ago this Friday,

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:55.730
the Minetas,

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:58.717
along with more than
120,000 Japanese-Americans

00:04:58.717 --> 00:05:00.200
on the west coast,

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.350
were forced from their
homes and communities,

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.254
and relocated into camps

00:05:04.254 --> 00:05:09.254
operated by the newly established
War Relocation Authority.

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.790
The Minetas along with
their friends and neighbors,

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:14.120
ended up at Santa Anita Racetrack,

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:17.293
and then moved onto Heart
Mountain in Wyoming.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:22.270
In 1943, his father received WRA clearance

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:24.720
to leave to teach Japanese language

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:26.920
in the military training facility

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:31.100
connected to Northwestern
University in Evanston, Illinois,

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:33.430
just north of Chicago.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.622
The family would eventually
be allowed to join him,

00:05:35.622 --> 00:05:38.272
and Norman Mineta would
spend his middle school years

00:05:38.272 --> 00:05:41.570
attending school in Evanston.

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:43.943
So maybe we can claim
you as a Midwesterner.

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:46.410
Following the war,

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:49.230
the Minetas joined many west
coast Japanese-Americans

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:53.480
and returned home to rebuild their lives.

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:55.540
Norman Mineta continued his education,

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:57.750
graduating from San Jose High School,

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.610
and the University of
California at Berkeley,

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:01.650
where he participated

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:04.730
in the reserve officers training program.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:07.516
He served as an Intelligence
Officer with the US Army

00:06:07.516 --> 00:06:09.210
during the 1950s,

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.517
before joining his
father's insurance business

00:06:11.517 --> 00:06:13.253
back in San Jose.

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:16.280
Norman Mineta's path to public service

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:17.720
began with his involvement

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.070
in the local Japanese-American
community in San Jose.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:23.720
In 1962 he was appointed to the San Jose

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:25.430
Human Relations Commission,

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:29.740
and then to the Municipal
Housing Authority Board in 1966.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:31.925
He then served on the
San Jose City Council.

00:06:31.925 --> 00:06:36.180
In 1971 he was elected Mayor of San Jose,

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.430
becoming the first Asian
American to hold this office

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:42.130
in a major American city.

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:45.840
In 1974 he was elected to the
US House of Representatives

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.911
for California's 13th District.

00:06:47.911 --> 00:06:51.730
His service to this district
lasted for 21 years,

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:56.540
where he either sponsored
or co-sponsored 479 bills,

00:06:56.540 --> 00:07:00.250
including the Americans with
Disabilities Act of 1991,

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:05.050
and the Intermodal Transportation
Efficiency Act of 1991.

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.250
He also played a crucial leadership role

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:11.240
in the landmark passage of the
Civil Liberties Act of 1988,

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:13.131
working closely with Japanese-American

00:07:13.131 --> 00:07:15.748
and Asian American community advocates,

00:07:15.748 --> 00:07:18.097
and civil rights activists,

00:07:18.097 --> 00:07:20.460
and lobbying fellow Congressmen

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:24.202
to redress the unjust
incarceration of Japanese-Americans

00:07:24.202 --> 00:07:25.713
during World War II.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:28.671
In 1994, he co-founded

00:07:28.671 --> 00:07:31.990
the Congressional Asian
Pacific American Caucus,

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:33.863
and served as the first Chair.

00:07:33.863 --> 00:07:36.909
And after stepping down
from his Congressional seat,

00:07:36.909 --> 00:07:41.080
he took a brief vacation
from public service.

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:42.971
No, he didn't (laughing), not really.

00:07:42.971 --> 00:07:45.503
He assumed the position of Vice President

00:07:45.503 --> 00:07:47.820
of Lockheed Martin Corporation,

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:48.860
and became the Chairman

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:52.490
of the Nation Civil
Aviation Review Commission.

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:54.480
During the second presidential term

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:56.810
of President William J. Clinton,

00:07:56.810 --> 00:08:00.630
Norman Mineta was called
back into public service

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:02.330
as a member of his Cabinet,

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:05.300
serving as the Secretary of Commerce.

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.550
This appointment made him
the first Asian American

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:10.220
to serve on a Presidential Cabinet.

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:11.743
And under George W. Bush,

00:08:11.743 --> 00:08:15.380
Norman Mineta became the
Secretary of Transportation,

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:18.960
and oversaw the grounding
of airplanes in US airspace,

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.700
shortly after the third
plane attack on the Pentagon.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:23.601
Under his term,

00:08:23.601 --> 00:08:24.980
he began the creation

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:28.450
of the Transportation Security
Administration, or TSA,

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:29.409
before it's relocation

00:08:29.409 --> 00:08:33.093
to the Department of
Homeland Security in 2003.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:37.610
If any of you have spent
time in the San Jose area,

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:41.051
probably more familiar to
all of you as Silicon Valley,

00:08:41.051 --> 00:08:43.570
you can see, still, very much,

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:47.030
how much his service
continues to be celebrated.

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:48.069
You've probably landed

00:08:48.069 --> 00:08:52.099
in Norman Y. Mineta International Airport.

00:08:52.099 --> 00:08:55.959
You may have driven on
California State Highway 85,

00:08:55.959 --> 00:09:00.959
also named after Norman Y. Mineta.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:04.840
As students who are from San Jose tell me,

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:07.900
you really cannot walk
around and not notice

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:09.850
Norman Y. Minetta's name (chuckling),

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:12.070
on just about everything.

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:15.730
In addition, San Jose State University

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:18.680
built the Norman Mineta
Transportation Institute,

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:21.192
that supports design
and policy innovations

00:09:21.192 --> 00:09:23.760
and sustainability and governance,

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.133
for local and national
transportation infrastructures.

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:31.330
He stepped down from his
Cabinet position in 2006,

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:34.100
and was awarded the
Presidential Medal of Freedom

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.760
by President George W. Bush.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:41.370
In 2007, he received the
Grand Order of the Rising Sun,

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:43.430
by the government of Japan.

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:46.730
And in 2012, he received the
distinguished Medal of Honor

00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:49.023
for Lifetime Achievement
and Public Service,

00:09:49.023 --> 00:09:51.903
from the Japanese-American
National Museum.

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:56.900
And now it is my extreme honor
and pleasure and privilege,

00:09:56.900 --> 00:10:01.230
that he has accepted the
Chubb Fellowship in 2016.

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:03.320
So please join me in welcoming

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:06.925
the Honorable Norman Y. Mineta
to the stage (applauding).

00:10:06.925 --> 00:10:10.092
(audience applauding)

00:10:30.902 --> 00:10:35.220
- Mary, thank you very
(throat clearing) very much

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:38.783
for that warm and generous introduction.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:46.373
And thanks to all of you
for the very warm welcome,

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:50.533
despite the weather as it is outside.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:55.920
You cannot imagine how thrilled I am

00:10:55.920 --> 00:11:00.920
to be honored as the Chubb
Fellow here at Yale University

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:04.473
and Timothy Dwight College.

00:11:07.588 --> 00:11:11.960
When I was at San Jose
High School graduating,

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:16.057
my best friend came
here to Yale University,

00:11:16.057 --> 00:11:17.543
and I went to Berkeley.

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:19.857
And pretty soon he wrote to me and said,

00:11:19.857 --> 00:11:22.817
"Well, I'm here at
Timothy Dwight College."

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:24.637
I wrote back to him, I said,

00:11:24.637 --> 00:11:26.188
"Bruce, I thought you were at Yale."

00:11:26.188 --> 00:11:28.180
(audience laughing)

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:32.103
Little did I know about the
residential college program.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:38.120
And yet, about three
years ago, a number of us,

00:11:40.670 --> 00:11:45.390
having lived at a certain
dormitory at Berkeley,

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:49.180
decided that we wanted to take it over

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:51.300
in a public/private partnership,

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:54.410
and create a residential college program.

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:56.663
So we raised some $47 million,

00:11:57.730 --> 00:12:02.730
and last year we were able
to get a 75 year lease

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:05.390
from the University of California,

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:08.480
and we are patterning the reconstruction

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:13.480
and operation of Bowles Hall at Berkeley

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:16.917
after the residential college program

00:12:16.917 --> 00:12:19.333
of the Ivy League schools.

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:24.590
We found a lot of resistance
when were were doing this,

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:29.590
until Nicholas Dirks from
Columbia University, the Provost,

00:12:30.573 --> 00:12:34.970
became the Chancellor at Berkeley.

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:36.980
And when we took the idea to him,

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:40.570
he immediately took to the idea

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:43.827
of creating a residential college program.

00:12:43.827 --> 00:12:46.390
And so this September,

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:50.613
we will be opening up
Bowles Hall as a RSP,

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:57.250
patterned after the
residential college program

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:00.990
of the Ivy League schools.

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:05.100
And Chancellor Dirks has also indicated

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:09.630
that he's gonna be tearing
down the 11 dormitories

00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:12.060
that we have at Berkeley.

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:17.060
He says they look like
Soviet-style stalag creations.

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:24.360
So he wants to take those down

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:28.890
and build them on a
residential college program.

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:29.960
But I am really thrilled,

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:32.470
because Bruce was my closest friend,

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:36.470
and unfortunately passed
away earlier this year.

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:41.033
Was a resident here at
Timothy Dwight College.

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:45.900
Then after graduating
and serving in the Navy,

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:50.070
he came back and graduated
from the School of Law

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.920
here at Yale.

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:57.920
But in any event, I am
just thrilled to be here

00:13:58.260 --> 00:14:03.183
as the Chubbs Fellow for 2016.

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:06.720
First of all I'd like to
introduce my wife, Deni,

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:08.060
who's here in front.

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:09.658
Honey, you wanna say hi?

00:14:09.658 --> 00:14:12.825
(audience applauding)

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:16.893
If you don't mind,

00:14:16.893 --> 00:14:21.640
I am going to use a lot
of this presentation

00:14:22.578 --> 00:14:27.200
as a personal experience
that I encountered,

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:30.280
even though this has been the experience

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:34.093
of many, many people of Japanese ancestry.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:39.120
My parents both immigrated from Japan.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:43.960
My dad in 1902 as a 14-year-older

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:46.623
came to the United States on his own.

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:49.500
And I always think,

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:54.500
would I ever have let my 14-year-old sons

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:57.640
go off to a country where
they don't know the language,

00:14:57.640 --> 00:14:59.220
they don't know anybody,

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:02.220
and just started life anew.

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:05.300
But my dad was the number two son.

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:07.030
Everything goes to number one.

00:15:07.030 --> 00:15:10.670
So he figured, I might as
well go and see what it's like

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:12.510
in the United States.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:15.920
And as he said, he didn't know
that much about US geography.

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:19.150
Instead of getting of of
the ship in San Francisco,

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:21.750
he got off the ship in Seattle.

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:26.563
So he was 800, 900 miles
away from where he should be.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.980
But he worked from one lumber camp,

00:15:29.980 --> 00:15:32.387
from a farm camp, to
another (throat clearing),

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:37.800
to make his way all the
way down to Salinas,

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:40.210
where his uncle had started to work

00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:42.207
for Spreckels Sugar Company (coughing).

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:47.540
I'm sorry, but I'm

00:15:51.700 --> 00:15:53.800
just on the tail end of a cold (coughing).

00:15:56.870 --> 00:15:58.400
So you'll have to excuse me

00:16:00.129 --> 00:16:02.747
if my voice gives out
every so often (coughing).

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:06.980
And I'm more sympathetic

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:10.730
to the person who's handing
the audio back here,

00:16:10.730 --> 00:16:12.520
listening to my coughing

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:16.117
through the mike (coughing).
(audience laughing)

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.573
So his uncle said, when he got to Salinas,

00:16:32.257 --> 00:16:33.867
"You've got to learn English."

00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:39.943
So he put him in the first grade.

00:16:42.116 --> 00:16:44.300
And he said it was the
most humiliating thing

00:16:44.300 --> 00:16:45.967
he has ever done (coughing).

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:51.130
Not because he didn't know English,

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:53.220
but he was five foot two.

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:54.540
And the kids in that grammar school

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:56.943
were getting up as tall as he was.

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:01.390
So he said that became
a very great incentive

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:03.190
for him to learn English (coughing).

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:08.582
By the end of the second grade,

00:17:08.582 --> 00:17:12.820
he had decided he had
learned enough English,

00:17:12.820 --> 00:17:17.203
so he graduated himself
from the second grade.

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:25.083
And then Spreckels Sugar gave him a job.

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:30.487
So he was very thankful
for that (coughing).

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:45.070
My mother came in 1914,

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:49.810
and she was a picture bride.

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:53.080
'Cause when my dad turned
24 he wrote to a friend,

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:56.823
and said, "You know, it's
time for me to get married,

00:17:59.477 --> 00:18:01.487
"but there are no Japanese women here."

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:08.690
So the friend wrote, sent
him a bunch of pictures,

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:12.497
and said, you know, here's
Mary, Suzie, Joanne (coughing).

00:18:15.390 --> 00:18:16.690
And he said, "I'm also..."

00:18:17.635 --> 00:18:19.620
(coughing)

00:18:19.620 --> 00:18:20.453
Excuse me.

00:18:22.217 --> 00:18:24.213
"Enclosing a picture of my sister.

00:18:25.587 --> 00:18:28.667
"Remember when we used to
tease her and kick her around

00:18:28.667 --> 00:18:30.207
"when we were growing up."

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:35.143
So my dad decided to marry his sister.

00:18:36.230 --> 00:18:38.480
But he hadn't seen her
for a number of years.

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:42.930
So she was known as part of that group,

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:44.453
known as picture brides.

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:51.423
And they got married in
San Francisco in 1914.

00:18:56.821 --> 00:18:59.071
(coughing)

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:04.380
I know this is tougher on you than me,

00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:06.003
and I apologize.

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:13.100
So let's fast forward to December 7, 1941.

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:17.443
Now, as Mary indicated,

00:19:19.060 --> 00:19:23.370
I was born on November 12, 1931,

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:27.693
and I chose very carefully the
family that I was born into.

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:31.050
A great mother, a great father,

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:34.220
three great older sisters,
and a great older brother,

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:36.600
and I was the youngest of five,

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:40.932
and could not have had a better upbringing

00:19:40.932 --> 00:19:42.563
than in that family.

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:47.190
But when December 7th occurred,

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:49.720
and my dad was in the insurance business

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:53.380
and well known in the Japanese community,

00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:55.607
and because he knew English,

00:19:55.607 --> 00:19:58.660
he sort of became the liaison

00:19:58.660 --> 00:20:02.136
between the Japanese community
and the majority community

00:20:02.136 --> 00:20:03.773
in San Jose.

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:08.523
And so, we had just
returned home from church,

00:20:08.523 --> 00:20:12.150
past noon, on that Sunday,

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:13.740
turned on the radio,

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:17.883
then heard all the news about
what was happening in Hawaii.

00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:22.060
And people started calling the house,

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:25.450
wondering, given what's
happening in Hawaii,

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:28.330
what's the impact going to be on us.

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:30.890
And then about 1:30, two o'clock,

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:33.290
Joyce Hirano, our next door neighbor,

00:20:33.290 --> 00:20:35.597
comes running in through
the back door yelling,

00:20:35.597 --> 00:20:36.927
"The police are taking Papa away,

00:20:36.927 --> 00:20:39.420
"the police are taking Papa away!"

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:43.675
So my dad runs out the
front door, next door,

00:20:43.675 --> 00:20:44.800
and by the time he got there,

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:48.430
Mr. Hirano was already gone.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:53.260
He had no idea, and Mrs.
Hirano, nor their son Irving,

00:20:53.260 --> 00:20:55.710
knew who came to the house.

00:20:55.710 --> 00:20:58.711
But Mr. Hirano was gone.

00:20:58.711 --> 00:21:00.730
So my dad came home

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:03.604
and he called the city manager of San Jose

00:21:03.604 --> 00:21:05.623
to ask what was going on.

00:21:08.111 --> 00:21:09.573
Mr. Goodwin says,

00:21:10.407 --> 00:21:13.440
"I have no idea what
you're talking about."

00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:16.570
So he said, "But why don't
you call Chief Black."

00:21:16.570 --> 00:21:19.777
So he called Chief of
Police Black, and said,

00:21:19.777 --> 00:21:20.640
"What's going on?"

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:22.357
And Chief Black said,

00:21:22.357 --> 00:21:25.527
"I don't know anything about
what you're talking about.

00:21:25.527 --> 00:21:27.720
"Why don't you talk to Sheriff Emig."

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:29.920
So he talked to Sheriff Emig,

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:31.431
and Sheriff Emig said,

00:21:31.431 --> 00:21:33.047
"Well, it's not my operation,

00:21:33.047 --> 00:21:34.557
"but I do know about it.

00:21:34.557 --> 00:21:39.033
"It's the FBI conducting this operation.

00:21:40.207 --> 00:21:44.910
"And I'll have the FBI give you a call."

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:47.880
So about 4:30 that afternoon,

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:52.737
the FBI, the special agent in
charge of the San Jose office

00:21:52.737 --> 00:21:54.730
came to the house.

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:57.490
And he explained that they
were picking up people

00:21:57.490 --> 00:22:00.140
who were sympathetic to the Japanese,

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:03.483
and who were also community leaders.

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:06.670
Well, my dad sot of though,

00:22:06.670 --> 00:22:09.000
I'm a community leader.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:12.127
Why am I not being picked up?

00:22:12.127 --> 00:22:14.350
And so he was a little insulted

00:22:14.350 --> 00:22:16.820
that he was not being looked at

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:20.290
as a potential person to be picked up.

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:23.250
And, in fact, after the FBI agent left,

00:22:23.250 --> 00:22:24.920
my mother and dad packed a suitcase

00:22:24.920 --> 00:22:27.600
just in case they came back,

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:29.323
which fortunately they did not.

00:22:30.980 --> 00:22:35.980
So, because of all this
transpiring at Pearl Harbor,

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:39.730
people were really concerned

00:22:39.730 --> 00:22:43.000
about what was going to happen to them.

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:45.530
And on the west coast at that time,

00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:48.760
there was a Western Civil Defense Command

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:53.760
that was headed up by a General DeWitt.

00:22:54.250 --> 00:22:59.250
And so, on February 19th, 1942,

00:23:04.360 --> 00:23:08.870
President Roosevelt signed
Executive Order 9066,

00:23:08.870 --> 00:23:11.690
delegating to the Commanding General

00:23:11.690 --> 00:23:13.910
of Western Civil Defense Command,

00:23:13.910 --> 00:23:16.930
the ability to evacuate persons.

00:23:16.930 --> 00:23:19.491
Didn't say Germans, didn't say Japanese,

00:23:19.491 --> 00:23:21.270
didn't say Italian;

00:23:21.270 --> 00:23:22.653
just said persons.

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:28.840
General DeWitt never did like
those of Japanese ancestry,

00:23:31.350 --> 00:23:33.740
and he used the phrase one time,

00:23:33.740 --> 00:23:38.010
once a J-A-P, always a J-A-P.

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:40.450
And he figured if the
Japanese were going to be able

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:42.970
to attack Pearl Harbor,

00:23:42.970 --> 00:23:45.050
they would be able to
attack the west coast.

00:23:45.050 --> 00:23:47.270
And if they attack the west coast,

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:51.850
what do I do with the 120,000
people of Japanese ancestry

00:23:51.850 --> 00:23:54.543
from Washington, Oregon, and California?

00:23:56.380 --> 00:24:00.540
And so, with Executive
Order 9066 (coughing)

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:01.753
now in his hand,

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:14.680
he then commandeered race
tracks and county fairgrounds,

00:24:15.009 --> 00:24:18.740
because they had build in living quarters,

00:24:18.740 --> 00:24:21.170
namely horse stables.

00:24:21.170 --> 00:24:25.380
And so (coughing) a number of fairgrounds,

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:28.920
in Washington, Oregon, and California,

00:24:28.920 --> 00:24:31.120
including (coughing)
race tracks (coughing),

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:48.463
were taken over by the
Western Civil Defense Command.

00:24:52.630 --> 00:24:54.250
And in order to notify us

00:24:56.331 --> 00:24:58.681
that we were going to be
subject to evaluation,

00:24:59.766 --> 00:25:01.381
they had these big placards,

00:25:01.381 --> 00:25:04.720
and they posted them on
the side of buildings,

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:05.943
utility poles.

00:25:07.150 --> 00:25:10.174
Attention, all those of Japanese ancestry,

00:25:10.174 --> 00:25:12.763
alien and non-alien.

00:25:14.859 --> 00:25:17.390
And as a 10-year-old
kid as I looked at that,

00:25:17.390 --> 00:25:20.403
I'm going, alien an non-alien.

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:23.490
Who's the non-alien?

00:25:23.490 --> 00:25:24.937
So I asked my brother, I said,

00:25:24.937 --> 00:25:27.150
"Who's a non-alien."

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:29.350
He said, "That's you, a citizen."

00:25:29.350 --> 00:25:31.647
I said, "Well why aren't
they calling us citizens

00:25:31.647 --> 00:25:33.095
"instead of non-aliens?"

00:25:33.095 --> 00:25:36.290
But already the psychological warfare

00:25:36.290 --> 00:25:37.623
was being played on us.

00:25:39.020 --> 00:25:41.130
And that's why, to this day,

00:25:41.130 --> 00:25:44.220
I cherish the word citizen,

00:25:44.220 --> 00:25:49.220
because my own government
wasn't willing to use it on us,

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:53.490
and we were considered
non-aliens (throat clearing).

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:58.490
And then in April of 1942, my brother,

00:26:00.175 --> 00:26:05.175
and he was then a sophomore
at San Jose State College,

00:26:07.460 --> 00:26:11.230
and he and I shared a
bedroom, and he's crying.

00:26:11.230 --> 00:26:13.240
I say, "Al, what's the matter?"

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:17.797
He says, "Well, this is my draft card, 1A,

00:26:17.797 --> 00:26:19.517
"fit and able to serve.

00:26:19.517 --> 00:26:21.497
"But I got a new one yesterday,

00:26:21.497 --> 00:26:23.967
"and it says 4C.

00:26:23.967 --> 00:26:26.567
"So I just called the Draft Board today

00:26:26.567 --> 00:26:29.237
"to find out what's a 4C.

00:26:29.237 --> 00:26:31.100
"Enemy alien."

00:26:31.100 --> 00:26:34.324
So here's kid who was
born and raised, San Jose,

00:26:34.324 --> 00:26:36.390
who all of a sudden,

00:26:36.390 --> 00:26:41.390
as were all Japanese-American
males of draft age,

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:47.010
reclassified as enemy aliens.

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:51.350
So, with all this kinda
confusion and turmoil going,

00:26:51.350 --> 00:26:54.890
and anxiety going on in the community,

00:26:54.890 --> 00:26:56.380
everyone was having to get rid

00:26:56.380 --> 00:26:59.983
of their personal and real properties,

00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:07.730
because you could only take
to camp what you could carry.

00:27:07.730 --> 00:27:11.210
And as I was saying to a
group of students earlier,

00:27:11.210 --> 00:27:16.180
the toughest job I had
was to part with my dog.

00:27:16.180 --> 00:27:19.975
And I loved peanut butter,
so my dog's name was Skippy.

00:27:19.975 --> 00:27:21.318
(audience laughing)

00:27:21.318 --> 00:27:22.550
And a little terrier.

00:27:22.550 --> 00:27:23.473
But I had to get rid of him,

00:27:23.473 --> 00:27:27.430
and it was the toughest
thing, I think, I've done,

00:27:27.430 --> 00:27:32.260
the least for a 10-year-old
it was a tough thing to do.

00:27:32.260 --> 00:27:37.260
So we could only pack to take
to camp what we could carry.

00:27:37.430 --> 00:27:39.700
Now, my mother, as an example,

00:27:39.700 --> 00:27:43.210
always had a fetish about ironing things,

00:27:43.210 --> 00:27:45.820
whether it's sheets or anything else.

00:27:45.820 --> 00:27:47.720
But she couldn't take her iron,

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:50.660
'cause that was a contraband article.

00:27:50.660 --> 00:27:55.660
AM radios, and all cameras,
were contraband articles.

00:27:55.950 --> 00:28:00.893
So we were only able to take
to camp what we could carry.

00:28:02.430 --> 00:28:05.111
So on the day that we
were leaving San Jose

00:28:05.111 --> 00:28:09.523
to board the trains to go
off to camp, May 29th, 1942,

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:14.610
we were boarding the
train at the freight yard;

00:28:14.610 --> 00:28:17.913
not at the passenger depot,
but at the freight yard.

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:20.930
But for me, that wasn't too bad,

00:28:20.930 --> 00:28:22.490
because the freight yard

00:28:22.490 --> 00:28:25.770
was five blocks from my grammar school,

00:28:25.770 --> 00:28:28.230
and were were gonna leave at one o'clock,

00:28:28.230 --> 00:28:30.872
and a lot of the kids,
during the lunch hour,

00:28:30.872 --> 00:28:35.330
came down to the train
to see their friends off.

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:38.960
So, to that extent, it was a plus.

00:28:38.960 --> 00:28:41.260
So we boarded the train,

00:28:41.260 --> 00:28:43.487
and I was wearing a Cub Scout uniform,

00:28:43.487 --> 00:28:47.290
carrying a baseball,
baseball glove, and a bat.

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:49.655
And as I was getting on the train,

00:28:49.655 --> 00:28:53.780
the MPs confiscated the bat,

00:28:53.780 --> 00:28:56.723
on the basis it could be
used as a lethal weapon.

00:28:57.719 --> 00:29:00.860
So I went running, crying, to my dad.

00:29:00.860 --> 00:29:02.827
And he said, "No, no,
that's alright, don't worry.

00:29:02.827 --> 00:29:05.440
"We'll get a replacement for that bat."

00:29:05.440 --> 00:29:06.290
Well we never did,

00:29:06.290 --> 00:29:10.450
because there were no stores
in Santa Anita at the camp.

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:14.683
So it was a long time before
I got my own bat back again.

00:29:16.610 --> 00:29:21.610
Now, because of this experience...

00:29:21.930 --> 00:29:26.400
Now, unfortunately when
we got to Santa Anita,

00:29:26.400 --> 00:29:29.600
we were one of the last
groups coming in to camp.

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:33.100
So by that time, all
the stables were filled.

00:29:37.630 --> 00:29:42.340
But I used to go visit my
friends in June and July,

00:29:42.340 --> 00:29:44.030
and you could just imagine what it's like

00:29:44.030 --> 00:29:46.010
in Southern California,

00:29:46.010 --> 00:29:50.500
with that heat beating
down on those stables.

00:29:50.500 --> 00:29:54.050
And I don't know how those
folks lived in those stables,

00:29:54.050 --> 00:29:56.170
given the stench.

00:29:56.170 --> 00:29:58.670
But in any event, we were fortunate enough

00:29:58.670 --> 00:30:02.570
to be able to be assigned to
one of the barracks buildings

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:06.170
that had been built in the parking lot.

00:30:06.170 --> 00:30:08.710
So we were there until, roughly,

00:30:08.710 --> 00:30:12.700
the end of November of 1942.

00:30:12.700 --> 00:30:13.760
And in the meantime,

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:16.720
they were building these more,

00:30:16.720 --> 00:30:20.760
quote, permanent, unquote, camps,

00:30:20.760 --> 00:30:25.760
in Arizona, Colorado, Arkansas,
Wyoming, Idaho, and Utah.

00:30:29.720 --> 00:30:34.630
And so the camp we went
to from Santa Anita,

00:30:34.630 --> 00:30:36.720
was Heart Mountain, Wyoming,

00:30:36.720 --> 00:30:41.450
which is about 20 miles
east of Cody, Wyoming,

00:30:41.450 --> 00:30:45.083
which all of you know
is about 50 miles east

00:30:45.083 --> 00:30:47.690
of Yellowstone National Park.

00:30:47.690 --> 00:30:51.620
So we were up there in that
northwest part of Wyoming.

00:30:54.950 --> 00:30:56.870
And the day we got there,

00:30:56.870 --> 00:30:58.590
the wind was blowing

00:30:58.590 --> 00:31:03.590
and all of us from California were just,

00:31:04.880 --> 00:31:07.270
couldn't get over how cold it was.

00:31:07.270 --> 00:31:09.601
Not only cold, but with that wind blowing,

00:31:09.601 --> 00:31:13.690
and that sand just
beating against your face,

00:31:13.690 --> 00:31:15.980
it just was something else.

00:31:15.980 --> 00:31:19.680
And so we get to our barracks building,

00:31:19.680 --> 00:31:22.080
and it's just filled with silt,

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:24.240
and no brooms or anything to use.

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:27.110
So we had to us whatever we could find

00:31:27.110 --> 00:31:31.950
to use as a shovel to sift,

00:31:31.950 --> 00:31:35.203
or get this sand off to the side.

00:31:36.110 --> 00:31:40.220
And schools hadn't been built yet.

00:31:40.220 --> 00:31:41.950
And the elders were wondering,

00:31:41.950 --> 00:31:43.920
gee, what do we do with the kids?

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:45.420
So they had written to the Boy Scouts

00:31:45.420 --> 00:31:47.447
and the Girl Scouts of America, said,

00:31:47.447 --> 00:31:48.897
"Please come to the camps

00:31:48.897 --> 00:31:50.960
"and organize troops for us,"

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:52.200
which they did.

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:57.200
So within our camp we had, maybe
10 or 12, Boy Scout troops.

00:31:59.380 --> 00:32:04.380
And so we were having
jamborees in the camp.

00:32:05.380 --> 00:32:08.290
And our scout leaders
would write to the Scouts

00:32:08.290 --> 00:32:12.440
in Cody and Ralston and Deaver,

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:14.410
and some of those other communities

00:32:15.270 --> 00:32:16.800
that were surrounding the camp,

00:32:16.800 --> 00:32:17.957
and they'd say, "Come on in

00:32:17.957 --> 00:32:20.830
"and join us for our jamboree."

00:32:20.830 --> 00:32:21.907
And they'd write back and say,

00:32:21.907 --> 00:32:22.740
"No, no.

00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:24.367
"We're not going in there.

00:32:24.367 --> 00:32:26.317
"There's barbed wire around the camp,

00:32:26.317 --> 00:32:28.187
"there're military guard towers

00:32:28.187 --> 00:32:32.417
"with search lights and machine
gun mounts every 300 feet.

00:32:32.417 --> 00:32:35.200
"They're POWs, we're not going in there."

00:32:35.200 --> 00:32:37.127
And they'd write back
and say, "No, no, no.

00:32:37.127 --> 00:32:38.559
"These are not POWs.

00:32:38.559 --> 00:32:40.997
"These are Boy Scouts of America.

00:32:40.997 --> 00:32:43.277
"They wear the same uniform you do.

00:32:43.277 --> 00:32:45.939
"They read the same manual you do.

00:32:45.939 --> 00:32:49.447
"They go after the same
merit badges you do."

00:32:50.630 --> 00:32:54.930
Finally one of the
troops, Boy Scout Troops,

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:57.840
from Cody, Wyoming came in.

00:32:57.840 --> 00:33:01.210
So we had our knot tying contests,

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:03.370
we had our woodworking contests,

00:33:03.370 --> 00:33:07.923
we had those how to start a
fire without a match contests.

00:33:09.910 --> 00:33:12.150
And after we went through all of that,

00:33:12.150 --> 00:33:13.900
we then got paired off

00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:17.280
with a kid from the
Boy Scout troop in Cody

00:33:17.280 --> 00:33:18.463
to put up our tent.

00:33:23.540 --> 00:33:26.830
So this kid and I put up our tent.

00:33:26.830 --> 00:33:30.150
And Wyoming, it could
rain any time, any amount,

00:33:30.150 --> 00:33:35.150
so you have to build a moat
around the tent to protect it.

00:33:37.062 --> 00:33:40.580
So as we were digging
our moat around the tent,

00:33:40.580 --> 00:33:42.217
the kid from the Cody troop said,

00:33:42.217 --> 00:33:46.537
"There's a kid in my troop
in that tent below us.

00:33:46.537 --> 00:33:48.522
"I don't really care for him.

00:33:48.522 --> 00:33:51.017
"Would you mind if we cut the water

00:33:51.017 --> 00:33:53.246
"to exit that way if it rains?"

00:33:53.246 --> 00:33:58.246
(audience laughing)
(coughing)

00:33:58.320 --> 00:34:00.413
It was really no skin off my nose.

00:34:01.332 --> 00:34:03.270
(audience laughing)

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:04.447
So I said, "Sure."

00:34:05.470 --> 00:34:07.120
And, as luck would have it,

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:09.040
it started raining later,

00:34:09.040 --> 00:34:11.430
and our moat worked perfectly.

00:34:11.430 --> 00:34:13.960
The water drained down that way,

00:34:13.960 --> 00:34:16.590
and during the course of the evening,

00:34:16.590 --> 00:34:19.940
tent pegs pulled and tent came down.

00:34:19.940 --> 00:34:22.097
The kids in the tent with me
going (imitating laughing),

00:34:24.510 --> 00:34:26.710
just laughing all night long.

00:34:26.710 --> 00:34:27.617
And I finally said to him,

00:34:27.617 --> 00:34:30.540
"Alan, would you please shut
up so we get some sleep?"

00:34:30.540 --> 00:34:32.190
(audience laughing)

00:34:32.190 --> 00:34:33.973
His name was Alan Simpson.

00:34:35.990 --> 00:34:37.053
Sound familiar?

00:34:38.020 --> 00:34:43.020
Became the US Senator
from Wyoming in 1978.

00:34:43.230 --> 00:34:45.460
And the odd thing about it

00:34:45.460 --> 00:34:48.420
is that through junior high school,

00:34:48.420 --> 00:34:50.570
high school, and college,

00:34:50.570 --> 00:34:52.400
we wrote to each other.

00:34:52.400 --> 00:34:55.650
And then in '74 I got
elected to the House,

00:34:55.650 --> 00:34:59.023
and he got elected to the Senate '78.

00:34:59.990 --> 00:35:04.260
And even as conservative
and Republican he was,

00:35:04.260 --> 00:35:06.600
as liberal and Democrat as I was,

00:35:06.600 --> 00:35:08.150
we were the best of friends.

00:35:08.150 --> 00:35:10.653
And we are still to today.

00:35:18.380 --> 00:35:20.153
Let's go to 9/11.

00:35:21.210 --> 00:35:23.910
I'm Secretary of Transportation,

00:35:23.910 --> 00:35:25.470
and I'm having breakfast that morning

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:28.840
with the Deputy Prime Minister of Belgium,

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:33.040
who also serves as the
Minister of Transport,

00:35:33.040 --> 00:35:38.040
and with Jane Garvey,
who was head of the FAA.

00:35:38.240 --> 00:35:39.860
And the three of us were having breakfast,

00:35:39.860 --> 00:35:44.860
talking about aviation issues
before the European Union.

00:35:46.190 --> 00:35:47.960
And my Chief of Staff came in and said,

00:35:47.960 --> 00:35:50.137
"Secretary, may I see you?"

00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:54.880
So I excused myself and
went into my office.

00:35:54.880 --> 00:35:56.344
And as I went into my office,

00:35:56.344 --> 00:35:59.118
at the other end is a TV console.

00:35:59.118 --> 00:36:00.985
There were the twin towers,

00:36:00.985 --> 00:36:04.900
black smoke billowing out of one of them.

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:06.450
I said, "John, what's that?"

00:36:06.450 --> 00:36:08.327
He said, "We don't know.

00:36:08.327 --> 00:36:10.907
"We've heard the possibility
of general aviation,

00:36:10.907 --> 00:36:13.517
"aircraft, commercial aircraft,

00:36:13.517 --> 00:36:14.777
"and even the possibility

00:36:14.777 --> 00:36:17.647
"of an explosion within the building."

00:36:19.060 --> 00:36:21.203
So I watch the TV for a little while,

00:36:23.607 --> 00:36:28.200
and then said, "I've got
to go into the meeting."

00:36:28.200 --> 00:36:30.410
So I went back in and explained to Ann,

00:36:30.410 --> 00:36:34.953
I mean to Jane, and to Mrs.
Durant what I had just seen.

00:36:36.470 --> 00:36:39.127
And six, seven minutes
later John came back in,

00:36:39.127 --> 00:36:40.370
"May I see you?"

00:36:40.370 --> 00:36:43.340
So I excused myself,
went back into my office.

00:36:43.340 --> 00:36:47.147
He said, "It's been confirmed
it was a commercial airliner

00:36:47.147 --> 00:36:49.060
"that went into the building."

00:36:49.060 --> 00:36:53.810
So as I was coming up
to the TV looking at it,

00:36:53.810 --> 00:36:57.570
all of a sudden I see this gray object

00:36:57.570 --> 00:36:59.600
going across the screen.

00:36:59.600 --> 00:37:03.120
And then on the left side
of the screen it disappears,

00:37:03.120 --> 00:37:06.415
and this white, yellow,
orangey, billowy cloud

00:37:06.415 --> 00:37:09.040
appears on the left side of the screen.

00:37:09.040 --> 00:37:11.282
And I go, "Holy cow, what was that?"

00:37:11.282 --> 00:37:14.220
Or words to that effect.
(audience chuckling)

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:18.380
And so then I really started to listen

00:37:18.380 --> 00:37:19.913
and look at the TV screen.

00:37:21.004 --> 00:37:25.207
So after about seven, eight
minutes of this I said,

00:37:25.207 --> 00:37:27.117
"John, I've gotta go back in,

00:37:27.117 --> 00:37:29.267
"get myself out of this
breakfast meeting."

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:36.910
So I went in, I explained to
Jane Garvey and Mrs. Durant

00:37:36.910 --> 00:37:39.453
what I had just seen
on the television set,

00:37:41.556 --> 00:37:43.907
and I said, "I don't know
what's going on up in New York,

00:37:43.907 --> 00:37:45.897
"but it's something I'm
gonna have to deal with.

00:37:45.897 --> 00:37:50.897
"Jane, you've gotta get back
to the FAA operation center."

00:37:50.900 --> 00:37:54.630
And so by the time I
got back into my office,

00:37:54.630 --> 00:37:56.247
the White House called and said,

00:37:56.247 --> 00:37:58.510
"Get over here right away."

00:37:58.510 --> 00:38:02.913
So I grab some papers and
threw them in my briefcase,

00:38:03.820 --> 00:38:05.600
and went downstairs to the car.

00:38:05.600 --> 00:38:08.320
And as we're going in West Executive Drive

00:38:08.320 --> 00:38:09.640
at the White House,

00:38:09.640 --> 00:38:12.270
people are running out of the
executive office building.

00:38:12.270 --> 00:38:14.190
They're running out of the White House.

00:38:14.190 --> 00:38:16.526
And I said to my driver and security guy,

00:38:16.526 --> 00:38:19.037
"Is there something
wrong with this picture?

00:38:19.037 --> 00:38:22.950
"We're driving in and everybody
else is running away."

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:24.500
So get out of the car,

00:38:24.500 --> 00:38:26.040
went into the White House.

00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:26.873
And the guard said,

00:38:26.873 --> 00:38:29.027
"You have to be briefed by Mr. Clarke

00:38:29.027 --> 00:38:30.800
"in the Situation Room."

00:38:30.800 --> 00:38:32.510
So I went into the Situation Room,

00:38:32.510 --> 00:38:35.617
and Dick briefed me.

00:38:35.617 --> 00:38:38.450
And he really didn't tell me much more

00:38:38.450 --> 00:38:42.090
than what I already had
heard on television.

00:38:42.090 --> 00:38:43.527
And then he said,

00:38:43.527 --> 00:38:45.238
"You've gotta be in the PEOC."

00:38:45.238 --> 00:38:47.277
I said, "The PEOC?

00:38:47.277 --> 00:38:48.489
"What's the PEOC?"

00:38:48.489 --> 00:38:50.850
P-E-O-C.

00:38:50.850 --> 00:38:53.800
Presidential Emergency Operation Center.

00:38:53.800 --> 00:38:58.630
And I said, "I have no
idea where that is."

00:38:58.630 --> 00:39:01.250
And there was a secret
service agent standing there.

00:39:01.250 --> 00:39:02.540
So he said, "I'll take you down."

00:39:02.540 --> 00:39:06.210
Now, this is a bunker that's
way under the White House.

00:39:06.210 --> 00:39:10.430
And it's supposed to be
nuclear bomb proof I guess.

00:39:10.430 --> 00:39:12.287
But in any event, I went down there,

00:39:12.287 --> 00:39:17.150
and the Vice President
was already down there.

00:39:17.150 --> 00:39:18.470
And there's a table,

00:39:18.470 --> 00:39:21.990
probably about 30, 40 feet long,

00:39:21.990 --> 00:39:24.013
about 15 feet wide,

00:39:24.013 --> 00:39:26.580
with chairs all the way around.

00:39:26.580 --> 00:39:29.930
And between each of the
chairs are telephones.

00:39:29.930 --> 00:39:34.437
So I took one set of phone, one phone,

00:39:34.437 --> 00:39:36.148
and set it up to my office

00:39:36.148 --> 00:39:38.800
at the Department of Transportation,

00:39:38.800 --> 00:39:41.010
and the other one to the FAA,

00:39:41.010 --> 00:39:46.010
and sat there in the PEOC
for the rest of the day.

00:39:52.440 --> 00:39:53.640
A military aide came in,

00:39:53.640 --> 00:39:56.503
to the Vice President who
was sitting across from me,

00:39:57.340 --> 00:40:00.990
said, "There's a plane coming towards DC."

00:40:00.990 --> 00:40:05.280
So I said to Monte Belger,
the number two at FAA,

00:40:05.280 --> 00:40:07.957
I said, "Monte, I
understand there's a plane

00:40:07.957 --> 00:40:09.207
"coming towards DC.

00:40:09.207 --> 00:40:14.057
"Do you know where it is
and what's the plane?"

00:40:15.400 --> 00:40:16.947
Said, "Well, we've been
tracking this plane,

00:40:16.947 --> 00:40:20.017
"but the transponder's been turned off.

00:40:20.017 --> 00:40:25.017
"So all we're doing is watching
the white little target

00:40:25.277 --> 00:40:30.277
"on every 11 second swing of the radar."

00:40:30.380 --> 00:40:34.327
So, now I said, "Looking at radar,

00:40:34.327 --> 00:40:38.100
"can you relate it to
where it's on the ground?"

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:40.030
Which is difficult to do.

00:40:40.030 --> 00:40:45.030
He said, "No, it's somewhere
in the middle of Pennsylvania."

00:40:45.180 --> 00:40:47.277
So every so often I'd be asking,

00:40:47.277 --> 00:40:48.730
"Where's the plane?"

00:40:48.730 --> 00:40:50.780
And so he would sorta guess

00:40:50.780 --> 00:40:53.143
as to where he thought the plane was.

00:40:54.900 --> 00:40:57.113
Well, on my credenza behind my desk,

00:41:02.480 --> 00:41:07.300
I had a monitor outline of the 48 States,

00:41:07.300 --> 00:41:09.660
Hawaii, and Alaska.

00:41:09.660 --> 00:41:11.580
Just peppered with dots.

00:41:11.580 --> 00:41:13.993
Well, if I took my mouse
and put it on a dot,

00:41:14.970 --> 00:41:18.270
all of a sudden there'd be
a flag that would come up,

00:41:18.270 --> 00:41:20.377
and it would say UA123 (coughing).

00:41:24.410 --> 00:41:26.393
United Airlines flight 123.

00:41:28.189 --> 00:41:29.640
And then it would say PDX

00:41:29.640 --> 00:41:32.573
and a number of navigational
points, and then ORD.

00:41:33.620 --> 00:41:35.490
So I knew it left Portland,

00:41:35.490 --> 00:41:37.910
this is the route it's gonna be flying,

00:41:37.910 --> 00:41:41.193
lands at O'Hare field in Chicago.

00:41:42.421 --> 00:41:44.113
And then the next line, it would say B752.

00:41:47.010 --> 00:41:50.253
Bowing 757, 200 series.

00:41:51.950 --> 00:41:55.040
Then it would give me the
compass direction of the plane,

00:41:55.040 --> 00:41:59.807
speed, tell me about how much
fuel (coughing) in the plane,

00:42:02.635 --> 00:42:05.163
tell me a lot about the airplane.

00:42:09.390 --> 00:42:13.230
And that was the transponder
that was turned off.

00:42:13.230 --> 00:42:15.742
So every so often I'd ask, "Where is it?"

00:42:15.742 --> 00:42:18.727
He said, "Well, I think
it's north of Baltimore."

00:42:20.207 --> 00:42:21.552
"Where is it now?"

00:42:21.552 --> 00:42:23.802
(coughing)

00:42:26.987 --> 00:42:29.360
"Probably near the
Great Falls (mumbling)."

00:42:35.254 --> 00:42:37.504
(coughing)

00:42:41.586 --> 00:42:42.767
"Where's it now?"

00:42:42.767 --> 00:42:46.027
"Oh, probably near Pentagon City

00:42:46.027 --> 00:42:48.647
"going towards National Airport."

00:42:49.857 --> 00:42:51.527
"Where is it now?"

00:42:51.527 --> 00:42:54.367
"Oops."
"Oops?

00:42:54.367 --> 00:42:55.497
"Oops what?"

00:42:57.267 --> 00:42:58.563
"We just lost the target.

00:43:02.647 --> 00:43:03.787
"We just lost the target.

00:43:03.787 --> 00:43:05.757
"We just lost the bogie."

00:43:05.757 --> 00:43:06.957
"Where'd you lose it?"

00:43:08.147 --> 00:43:13.147
"Somewhere between Pentagon
City National Airport."

00:43:16.184 --> 00:43:18.434
(coughing)

00:43:21.970 --> 00:43:24.140
And then about that time,

00:43:24.140 --> 00:43:26.317
someone broke into the phones and said,

00:43:26.317 --> 00:43:28.197
"Secretary, we just got a call

00:43:28.197 --> 00:43:30.097
"from an Arlington County Officer

00:43:30.097 --> 00:43:34.527
"who saw an American Airlines
go into the Pentagon."

00:43:35.930 --> 00:43:40.340
Well, Al-Queda used to always
talk about three icons.

00:43:40.340 --> 00:43:44.583
Military icon, political
icon, economic icon.

00:43:45.430 --> 00:43:49.610
And, obviously, that day
they hit the economic icon

00:43:49.610 --> 00:43:50.760
the World Trade Center.

00:43:52.970 --> 00:43:57.970
And then they hit the
military icon, the Pentagon.

00:43:59.100 --> 00:44:00.437
And I said to Monte, I said,

00:44:00.437 --> 00:44:03.797
"Monte, when you see
one of something happen,

00:44:03.797 --> 00:44:07.157
"especially when you see
three commercial airliners

00:44:07.157 --> 00:44:09.520
"being used as missiles."

00:44:09.520 --> 00:44:11.857
And I said, "Once you
see one of something,

00:44:11.857 --> 00:44:13.687
"it's an accident.

00:44:13.687 --> 00:44:17.537
"But if you see a second
of the same thing,

00:44:17.537 --> 00:44:20.413
"it's a pattern or a trend.

00:44:21.747 --> 00:44:24.087
"But when you see three of the same thing

00:44:24.087 --> 00:44:26.827
"in a relatively short period,

00:44:26.827 --> 00:44:29.090
"it's a plan or an action."

00:44:29.090 --> 00:44:30.067
And I said, "In the military,

00:44:30.067 --> 00:44:32.877
"they have something called a stand down.

00:44:32.877 --> 00:44:34.977
"And we've gotta do our own stand down.

00:44:34.977 --> 00:44:36.510
"Gotta bring all the planes down."

00:44:36.510 --> 00:44:39.337
And he said, "We'll
bring all the planes down

00:44:39.337 --> 00:44:40.887
"per pilot discretion."

00:44:40.887 --> 00:44:43.807
And I said, "Monte,
screw pilot discretion.

00:44:43.807 --> 00:44:45.630
"I want all the planes down."

00:44:45.630 --> 00:44:48.772
'Cause I didn't want someone
over Albuquerque or Phoenix

00:44:48.772 --> 00:44:51.210
go flying into Los Angeles.

00:44:51.210 --> 00:44:54.720
I wanted those planes
down as soon as possible.

00:44:54.720 --> 00:44:59.720
And at that point, we had
5,368 airplanes in the air.

00:45:00.580 --> 00:45:04.060
And in two hours and 20
minutes we had them all down,

00:45:04.060 --> 00:45:07.313
on the ground, safely,
and without incident.

00:45:08.250 --> 00:45:13.250
And that morning, I also
pulled three people out of ACS,

00:45:14.330 --> 00:45:19.120
Aviation Civil Security of the FAA.

00:45:19.120 --> 00:45:22.920
Started having them work
on a new security regimen.

00:45:22.920 --> 00:45:24.845
Because Tuesday afternoon,

00:45:24.845 --> 00:45:28.287
the CEOs of the airlines
were calling up, saying,

00:45:28.287 --> 00:45:30.280
"When are we gonna be able to fly."

00:45:30.280 --> 00:45:31.113
And I said to them,

00:45:31.113 --> 00:45:32.687
"You're not gonna be able to fly

00:45:32.687 --> 00:45:36.247
"until we establish a
new security regimen."

00:45:37.620 --> 00:45:41.730
So, as the folks were working
on that security regimen,

00:45:41.730 --> 00:45:44.770
on Thursday there was a Cabinet meeting

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:52.700
of the Cabinet with the member
of the House and Senate,

00:45:53.090 --> 00:45:56.580
Democratic and Republican leadership.

00:45:56.580 --> 00:45:58.620
And towards the end of that meeting,

00:45:58.620 --> 00:46:03.377
Congressman David Bonner,
from Detroit, Michigan, said,

00:46:03.377 --> 00:46:04.210
"Mr. President.

00:46:04.210 --> 00:46:07.977
"We have a very large
Arab-American population,

00:46:07.977 --> 00:46:10.877
"living in Michigan.

00:46:10.877 --> 00:46:11.837
"And they're very concerned

00:46:11.837 --> 00:46:15.127
"about all the rhetoric they're hearing

00:46:15.127 --> 00:46:18.447
"about banning Middle
Easterners from flying,

00:46:18.447 --> 00:46:20.467
"keeping Muslims off airplanes."

00:46:20.467 --> 00:46:21.943
One and on and on.

00:46:23.174 --> 00:46:24.675
And the President said,

00:46:24.675 --> 00:46:26.867
"David, you're absolutely right.

00:46:26.867 --> 00:46:30.727
"We are equally concerned
about all that rhetoric,

00:46:30.727 --> 00:46:33.147
"and we don't wanna have happen today

00:46:33.147 --> 00:46:36.247
"what happened to Norm in 1942."

00:46:37.270 --> 00:46:40.100
And so when I got back from the meeting,

00:46:40.100 --> 00:46:44.130
and asked how our security
regimen was coming along,

00:46:44.130 --> 00:46:46.780
they said the first one,
right at the top, was,

00:46:46.780 --> 00:46:49.563
no racial or ethnic profiling.

00:46:50.490 --> 00:46:55.490
And so, we were able to,
found out the rest of it,

00:46:55.980 --> 00:46:57.420
by late Friday afternoon,

00:46:57.420 --> 00:47:00.943
and issue that in the
form of a regulation.

00:47:03.040 --> 00:47:06.270
But again, it was a time in which

00:47:06.270 --> 00:47:08.849
there was a great deal of concern,

00:47:08.849 --> 00:47:13.849
about security, and about how to deal

00:47:15.750 --> 00:47:19.733
with an ethnic or racial minority,

00:47:20.610 --> 00:47:23.383
and, including, a religious group.

00:47:24.340 --> 00:47:27.730
And then towards the end
of September, in Arizona,

00:47:27.730 --> 00:47:30.890
there was a shooting
and killing of a person

00:47:30.890 --> 00:47:34.463
who owned a gas station and a mini mart.

00:47:35.670 --> 00:47:38.375
And when the apprehended
the killer, they asked him,

00:47:38.375 --> 00:47:40.603
why did you shoot and kill this guy?

00:47:41.560 --> 00:47:45.470
He said, simply, "Because
he looked like the enemy."

00:47:45.470 --> 00:47:47.870
This fellow happened to be a Sikh,

00:47:47.870 --> 00:47:52.870
with his head gear and
facial hair, leg bindings.

00:47:56.197 --> 00:47:59.873
And other than a simple explanation,

00:48:00.952 --> 00:48:02.763
'cause he looked like the enemy.

00:48:06.078 --> 00:48:11.078
On Monday the, I guess that'd
be the 17th of September,

00:48:11.650 --> 00:48:14.050
President gathered a large number

00:48:14.050 --> 00:48:16.930
of Arab-Americans and Muslims

00:48:16.930 --> 00:48:21.283
at the Islamic Studies
Center in Washington, DC.

00:48:22.240 --> 00:48:23.087
And he said,

00:48:23.087 --> 00:48:25.767
"We know who did that last Tuesday.

00:48:25.767 --> 00:48:28.417
"They were not loyal Arab-Americans.

00:48:28.417 --> 00:48:33.417
"They were not faithful
followers of Islam.

00:48:34.507 --> 00:48:36.307
"They were terrorists,

00:48:36.307 --> 00:48:38.640
"and we're gonna go after them."

00:48:38.640 --> 00:48:42.800
Similarly, with the killing of this Sikh.

00:48:42.800 --> 00:48:47.580
He had a very large group of
South Asian Indian Americans

00:48:47.580 --> 00:48:50.120
come to the White House in October,

00:48:50.120 --> 00:48:53.070
and he told them that
we are not gonna allow

00:48:53.070 --> 00:48:56.010
this kind of hate crime to occur,

00:48:56.010 --> 00:49:00.100
and we will pursue
whoever does these things,

00:49:00.100 --> 00:49:01.750
until they're brought to justice.

00:49:03.160 --> 00:49:05.020
So all of this, again,

00:49:05.020 --> 00:49:09.740
sort of relates to this December
7th, in terms of security

00:49:10.877 --> 00:49:15.877
and the need for weighing civil liberties,

00:49:18.550 --> 00:49:22.420
then 9/11, then now, today,

00:49:22.420 --> 00:49:24.523
with ISIS or ISIL.

00:49:25.730 --> 00:49:29.167
And all of us have to be very vigilant

00:49:29.167 --> 00:49:32.363
in the protection of our
constitutional rights.

00:49:33.308 --> 00:49:36.640
We don't have to be vigilantes about it,

00:49:36.640 --> 00:49:38.525
but we have to be very vigilant

00:49:38.525 --> 00:49:41.281
about our constitutional rights.

00:49:41.281 --> 00:49:42.710
And at the same time,

00:49:42.710 --> 00:49:45.961
I know there's a security concern

00:49:45.961 --> 00:49:49.271
that has to be attended to.

00:49:49.271 --> 00:49:53.660
I think in a democratic society of ours,

00:49:53.660 --> 00:49:57.150
that's something that
all of us are able to do,

00:49:57.150 --> 00:49:59.383
in an open and free discussion.

00:50:00.340 --> 00:50:03.390
But it's something that bothers me,

00:50:03.390 --> 00:50:06.680
because in this discussion

00:50:06.680 --> 00:50:11.680
of the political presidential
election run up to 2016,

00:50:14.400 --> 00:50:18.730
some things are being said
that really bother me,

00:50:18.730 --> 00:50:23.730
and sort of make me think
back to December 7, 1941,

00:50:23.740 --> 00:50:27.160
or 9/11/2001,

00:50:27.160 --> 00:50:32.160
or the present circumstance,
including I-S-I-S and I-S-I-L.

00:50:35.600 --> 00:50:39.526
So it's something that all
of us have to deal with.

00:50:39.526 --> 00:50:42.053
And to the young people in the audience,

00:50:44.010 --> 00:50:47.853
I want you to pursue your
career and professional goals.

00:50:48.920 --> 00:50:50.860
But as you do so,

00:50:50.860 --> 00:50:55.860
think about taking some
time to offer yourself

00:50:56.386 --> 00:50:59.090
as a subject matter expert

00:50:59.090 --> 00:51:02.270
to your Mayor, your county executive,

00:51:02.270 --> 00:51:04.283
Governor, or President.

00:51:05.370 --> 00:51:09.633
Because, as you pursue
your professional goal,

00:51:10.600 --> 00:51:13.700
I want you to also think
about what you can do

00:51:13.700 --> 00:51:18.700
to lend your subject matter
expertise to public policy,

00:51:20.590 --> 00:51:22.433
and help in that way.

00:51:23.740 --> 00:51:26.270
Not everybody has to run for office.

00:51:26.270 --> 00:51:27.690
In a democratic society,

00:51:27.690 --> 00:51:32.690
you have everyone from a well-read citizen

00:51:32.760 --> 00:51:35.930
to the person who runs for office.

00:51:35.930 --> 00:51:37.840
But the one who runs for office

00:51:37.840 --> 00:51:41.770
may be one or two percent
of the population.

00:51:41.770 --> 00:51:46.770
So you may not be in that
group that wants to run,

00:51:48.570 --> 00:51:52.020
but you want to do
something public service.

00:51:52.020 --> 00:51:56.450
And that's where you can still
pursue your own career goal,

00:51:56.450 --> 00:52:01.000
and you volunteer to be a member
of a Board or a commission

00:52:01.000 --> 00:52:04.110
to whatever level of government

00:52:04.110 --> 00:52:08.713
that you would like to lend yourself to.

00:52:09.550 --> 00:52:12.570
So with that, let me stop,

00:52:12.570 --> 00:52:16.490
and maybe take some
questions and answers, Mary,

00:52:16.490 --> 00:52:17.840
if that's all right?

00:52:17.840 --> 00:52:18.673
Okay?

00:52:18.673 --> 00:52:19.980
Thank you very much.

00:52:19.980 --> 00:52:23.147
(audience applauding)

00:52:33.330 --> 00:52:35.620
- So, I think this is on, yes this is on.

00:52:35.620 --> 00:52:37.910
So now, we are going to open it up

00:52:37.910 --> 00:52:39.340
for questions and answers.

00:52:39.340 --> 00:52:40.880
I believe when you all walked in

00:52:40.880 --> 00:52:42.800
you should have received a card,

00:52:42.800 --> 00:52:46.520
so please do write down
questions that you have

00:52:46.520 --> 00:52:48.807
for Secretary Mineta.

00:52:48.807 --> 00:52:50.170
And we do have aides

00:52:50.170 --> 00:52:52.750
who should be coming
up and down the aisles

00:52:52.750 --> 00:52:55.380
to collect your questions.

00:52:55.380 --> 00:52:57.380
So while that's happening,

00:52:57.380 --> 00:53:00.570
I do have a couple of quick
questions to start us off.

00:53:00.570 --> 00:53:01.480
Okay.

00:53:01.480 --> 00:53:03.590
The first one is,

00:53:03.590 --> 00:53:06.370
I was very struck by
the story that you told,

00:53:06.370 --> 00:53:09.320
and especially in terms
of the aftermath of 9/11,

00:53:09.320 --> 00:53:13.420
and the response by
President Bush at the time,

00:53:13.420 --> 00:53:17.270
saying absolutely no
racial or ethnic profiling,

00:53:17.270 --> 00:53:22.270
is just as important as the
security of the American people.

00:53:22.880 --> 00:53:24.740
So I wanted to hear a little bit more

00:53:24.740 --> 00:53:29.440
about how that sentiment
and that goal and vision

00:53:29.440 --> 00:53:32.346
was also part of the creation of the TSA

00:53:32.346 --> 00:53:34.620
under your leadership?

00:53:34.620 --> 00:53:36.626
- Next question (coughing)?

00:53:36.626 --> 00:53:40.440
(Mary laughing)
(audience laughing)

00:53:40.440 --> 00:53:44.283
Well, right after we had come into office,

00:53:45.354 --> 00:53:50.354
President Bush had invited
my wife and me to Camp David.

00:53:51.210 --> 00:53:55.010
And so after dinner one night,

00:53:55.010 --> 00:54:00.010
we had just sort of a
talk around the table,

00:54:00.572 --> 00:54:03.800
and I did explain, or talk about,

00:54:03.800 --> 00:54:04.633
'cause he did ask,

00:54:04.633 --> 00:54:08.180
"Well, tell me about the
evacuation and internment."

00:54:08.180 --> 00:54:09.710
We spent quite a bit of time

00:54:09.710 --> 00:54:11.900
about the evacuation and internment.

00:54:11.900 --> 00:54:16.900
And this would have been in,
probably about March of 2001.

00:54:21.493 --> 00:54:25.410
And so, the whole idea
of grounding the planes,

00:54:28.260 --> 00:54:33.260
really, I suppose if I
had to think about it,

00:54:33.650 --> 00:54:38.650
I don't know what I
would have done on 9/11,

00:54:38.770 --> 00:54:40.960
but I knew I was comfortable

00:54:40.960 --> 00:54:43.750
in having to order all the planes down.

00:54:43.750 --> 00:54:44.670
Now, the formation

00:54:44.670 --> 00:54:48.940
of the Transportation
Security Administration

00:54:48.940 --> 00:54:52.710
came because in the old system,

00:54:52.710 --> 00:54:54.360
the airlines were in charge

00:54:54.360 --> 00:54:59.360
of the inspection of
passengers onto airplanes.

00:55:01.310 --> 00:55:06.212
And they would contract
with independent companies

00:55:06.212 --> 00:55:09.300
to do this at all their airports.

00:55:09.300 --> 00:55:12.760
And, of course, those are
always done on the lowest bid,

00:55:12.760 --> 00:55:17.130
and given to the cheapest
contractor that submitted a bid.

00:55:17.130 --> 00:55:21.770
And so, obviously, 9/11 said to us

00:55:21.770 --> 00:55:25.460
there were a lot of flaws in the system.

00:55:25.460 --> 00:55:29.433
So we put together our own legislation,

00:55:31.360 --> 00:55:35.717
and the center of it was
that these inspectors

00:55:39.100 --> 00:55:41.540
were going to be government employees.

00:55:41.540 --> 00:55:45.480
And that became a very major arm wrestle

00:55:46.493 --> 00:55:50.625
between our department and the Congress

00:55:50.625 --> 00:55:52.710
as we're having

00:55:52.710 --> 00:55:57.040
the Transportation
Security Administration Act

00:55:57.040 --> 00:56:00.400
make its way through the House and Senate.

00:56:00.400 --> 00:56:01.478
'Cause many wanted to just keep going

00:56:01.478 --> 00:56:05.220
with private contractors.

00:56:05.220 --> 00:56:08.770
But I felt that in order
to have the same standard,

00:56:08.770 --> 00:56:12.700
whether you were in New
York or Resume Speed, Iowa,

00:56:12.700 --> 00:56:14.030
or wherever,

00:56:14.030 --> 00:56:18.100
to that it had to really
be a government employee,

00:56:18.100 --> 00:56:20.960
and we prevailed on that count.

00:56:20.960 --> 00:56:25.470
Now, we did include a provision
in that original bill,

00:56:25.470 --> 00:56:28.875
that allowed up to five communities

00:56:28.875 --> 00:56:33.875
to have outside contractors do the work.

00:56:34.210 --> 00:56:38.950
And we based it on large HUB carrier,

00:56:38.950 --> 00:56:43.237
small non-point carrier,

00:56:43.237 --> 00:56:46.210
and we had various varieties.

00:56:46.210 --> 00:56:49.237
But we did allow five airports

00:56:49.237 --> 00:56:53.320
to have non-government contract employees.

00:56:53.320 --> 00:56:58.320
But they have to have the same system

00:56:58.890 --> 00:57:01.930
as all the other government owned,

00:57:01.930 --> 00:57:05.983
or government operated security systems.

00:57:11.840 --> 00:57:14.003
Does that answer that Mary?

00:57:15.720 --> 00:57:17.980
- [Mary] So we have a lot of questions.

00:57:17.980 --> 00:57:19.860
And I do apologize if
we don't actually get

00:57:19.860 --> 00:57:20.693
to your question,

00:57:20.693 --> 00:57:22.369
I'm gonna do the best I can.

00:57:22.369 --> 00:57:23.900
One question is,

00:57:23.900 --> 00:57:26.010
can you talk about serving as a Democrat

00:57:26.010 --> 00:57:29.220
in a Republican administration's Cabinet.

00:57:29.220 --> 00:57:33.573
Considering the political
divisiveness in the country today,

00:57:35.910 --> 00:57:37.970
what advice might you have to offer,

00:57:37.970 --> 00:57:41.103
while we're on the partisan divides?

00:57:43.210 --> 00:57:44.327
- Well (throat clearing).

00:57:45.920 --> 00:57:48.240
This is really a difficult question

00:57:48.240 --> 00:57:51.343
because today it is,

00:57:52.250 --> 00:57:55.990
the way things operate in DC,

00:57:55.990 --> 00:57:59.010
totally different from what they were,

00:57:59.010 --> 00:58:02.633
even as recently as 15 years ago.

00:58:04.440 --> 00:58:05.273
Today...

00:58:05.273 --> 00:58:08.540
Well, let me go back to
when I was in the Congress.

00:58:08.540 --> 00:58:12.490
Tip O'Neill required us
to be there at 12 noon

00:58:12.490 --> 00:58:14.577
on Monday for the first vote,

00:58:14.577 --> 00:58:19.577
and then stay until the
last vote on Friday at 3pm.

00:58:20.620 --> 00:58:25.580
Today, the first vote
is on Tuesday at 6:30,

00:58:25.580 --> 00:58:29.200
so members come in late Tuesday afternoon,

00:58:29.200 --> 00:58:34.200
for a vote on, for their
6:30 vote on Tuesday.

00:58:34.260 --> 00:58:37.330
Then they're there on Wednesday.

00:58:37.330 --> 00:58:41.100
And the last vote is at
three o'clock on Thursday,

00:58:41.100 --> 00:58:42.880
if they have a vote.

00:58:42.880 --> 00:58:46.773
Now, in probably 90% of the days,

00:58:49.790 --> 00:58:54.790
or 90% of Thursdays, they
haven't had votes on Thursday.

00:58:55.800 --> 00:58:59.350
So they all leave early Thursday morning

00:58:59.350 --> 00:59:01.633
to go home to their home districts.

00:59:02.720 --> 00:59:04.403
They don't know each other,

00:59:05.260 --> 00:59:08.050
they don't know even the subject matter

00:59:08.050 --> 00:59:10.137
of the committees they're assigned to.

00:59:10.137 --> 00:59:13.210
And I still deal with many of the members,

00:59:13.210 --> 00:59:15.880
and it's appalling to me

00:59:15.880 --> 00:59:18.220
that they don't know the subject matter

00:59:18.220 --> 00:59:21.093
of the committees they're assigned to.

00:59:21.093 --> 00:59:22.903
On the other hand,

00:59:22.903 --> 00:59:27.560
running campaigns is so
much more expensive today

00:59:27.560 --> 00:59:30.413
than it was some time back.

00:59:32.000 --> 00:59:35.093
So they're all dialing
for dollars all the time.

00:59:36.020 --> 00:59:39.250
Now, in my first race in 1974,

00:59:39.250 --> 00:59:41.880
between the primary and
the general election,

00:59:41.880 --> 00:59:45.093
I spent $247,000.

00:59:46.060 --> 00:59:50.660
In my last race in 1994,
I raised $1.3 million,

00:59:51.550 --> 00:59:53.333
spent $700,000.

00:59:54.260 --> 00:59:59.213
Today, there isn't a race
that is under $2-2.5 million.

01:00:00.410 --> 01:00:05.000
And so they're having to
raise that money all the time.

01:00:05.000 --> 01:00:10.000
But what's even worse is the
fact that they're not in DC,

01:00:10.330 --> 01:00:12.680
they're not dealing with
their subject matters,

01:00:12.680 --> 01:00:14.470
they don't know each other.

01:00:14.470 --> 01:00:18.519
We used to have fights in sub-committee,

01:00:18.519 --> 01:00:21.190
full committee, on the floor,

01:00:21.190 --> 01:00:22.550
you'd slap each other on the back,

01:00:22.550 --> 01:00:24.477
say, "C'mon let's go have dinner,

01:00:24.477 --> 01:00:26.330
"let's go have a drink."

01:00:26.330 --> 01:00:27.780
And that doesn't occur today.

01:00:31.660 --> 01:00:36.380
There may be some isolated
examples of that happening.

01:00:36.380 --> 01:00:38.450
And so it makes it very difficult.

01:00:38.450 --> 01:00:39.870
And on top of that,

01:00:39.870 --> 01:00:44.650
the members themselves are
much more polarized today,

01:00:44.650 --> 01:00:47.960
and I think that part of it is,

01:00:47.960 --> 01:00:50.200
if you don't know somebody,

01:00:50.200 --> 01:00:54.940
you tend to be a little harsher on them.

01:00:54.940 --> 01:00:56.750
And I think that's what happens,

01:00:56.750 --> 01:00:59.410
is what's happening today.

01:00:59.410 --> 01:01:04.410
And there isn't that personal
relationship that existed.

01:01:07.210 --> 01:01:12.197
And as conservative as Alan Simpson was,

01:01:12.197 --> 01:01:14.400
and as liberal as I was,

01:01:14.400 --> 01:01:16.820
we'd still be able to have dinner,

01:01:16.820 --> 01:01:20.920
talk to each other about issues going on.

01:01:20.920 --> 01:01:23.443
And that really doesn't happen today.

01:01:27.330 --> 01:01:31.430
Now, there's a group of
bipartisan members of Congress,

01:01:31.430 --> 01:01:33.180
or former members,

01:01:33.180 --> 01:01:37.760
who are trying to work on
trying to get this corrected

01:01:37.760 --> 01:01:38.593
right now.

01:01:43.970 --> 01:01:47.070
- [Mary] I think of a, sort of,
a follow up question almost.

01:01:47.070 --> 01:01:48.350
Not exactly.

01:01:48.350 --> 01:01:50.600
But what was the most
significant difference

01:01:50.600 --> 01:01:53.510
between President Clinton and
President Bush (giggling).

01:01:53.510 --> 01:01:55.100
I'm not sure you can really answer that,

01:01:55.100 --> 01:01:57.130
but I can't resist to ask.
(audience laughing)

01:01:57.130 --> 01:01:59.803
I do say, I do have a real card here.

01:02:00.802 --> 01:02:01.913
- Well.

01:02:08.020 --> 01:02:12.533
President Bush was a CEO-type of person,

01:02:13.610 --> 01:02:16.573
where we, as Cabinet members.

01:02:17.520 --> 01:02:20.610
I remember when I spoke to him on,

01:02:20.610 --> 01:02:23.130
what was that, January 2nd I guess it was,

01:02:23.130 --> 01:02:27.500
when we went down to Austin to
meet him for the first time.

01:02:27.500 --> 01:02:29.410
And I had three things in mind.

01:02:29.410 --> 01:02:32.793
I wanna talk to him about
budget, policy, and personnel.

01:02:33.840 --> 01:02:35.193
So when I talked about budget he says,

01:02:35.193 --> 01:02:36.230
"No, no no."

01:02:36.230 --> 01:02:38.567
He says, "You're gonna have to arm wrestle

01:02:38.567 --> 01:02:43.257
"with everybody else at OMB
for your budget requirements."

01:02:44.980 --> 01:02:47.693
Then he said, on policy and personnel,

01:02:48.557 --> 01:02:52.057
"I'm bringing you on as
a subject matter expert.

01:02:52.057 --> 01:02:57.057
"So I want you to give to me
what you're policy advice is,

01:02:58.007 --> 01:03:01.317
"but I just don't you
want hiring only Democrats

01:03:01.317 --> 01:03:03.920
"at the Department of Transportation."

01:03:03.920 --> 01:03:04.753
And I said, "No, no.

01:03:04.753 --> 01:03:09.057
"I'll be using the
Presidential Personnel Office

01:03:09.057 --> 01:03:13.267
"to recruit and bring people in."

01:03:15.540 --> 01:03:19.600
So the President really was a delegator.

01:03:19.600 --> 01:03:22.487
He expected me to do my job.

01:03:26.820 --> 01:03:31.820
He wasn't overseeing what
I was doing or saying.

01:03:32.010 --> 01:03:34.603
Although we had to clear the speeches.

01:03:37.986 --> 01:03:40.320
But there was very little rewritten.

01:03:40.320 --> 01:03:41.730
President Clinton...

01:03:46.178 --> 01:03:48.400
Oh, and then Cabinet meetings.

01:03:48.400 --> 01:03:50.920
We were to have Cabinet
meetings every month.

01:03:50.920 --> 01:03:55.180
And if it was announced
to start at 10 o'clock,

01:03:55.180 --> 01:03:58.180
you knew right at the top of the hour,

01:03:58.180 --> 01:04:00.290
the meeting was going to start.

01:04:00.290 --> 01:04:02.260
And there was always an agenda,

01:04:02.260 --> 01:04:04.870
and it would always fall
under a certain pattern.

01:04:04.870 --> 01:04:08.330
Now, in the case of President Clinton,

01:04:08.330 --> 01:04:13.330
I came in as Secretary of
Commerce in June of 2000.

01:04:15.633 --> 01:04:18.880
Bill Daley was the Secretary of Commerce;

01:04:18.880 --> 01:04:22.060
he was leaving to go
run the Gore campaign.

01:04:22.060 --> 01:04:27.060
And I was Senior Vice
President at Lockheed Martin.

01:04:28.660 --> 01:04:32.570
Six o'clock, I had packed up my briefcase,

01:04:32.570 --> 01:04:34.100
I was about to leave,

01:04:34.100 --> 01:04:35.173
and the phone rang.

01:04:37.270 --> 01:04:40.310
Should I answer that phone, or let it go?

01:04:40.310 --> 01:04:42.140
Should I answer that phone,
(audience laughing)

01:04:42.140 --> 01:04:43.290
or let it go?

01:04:43.290 --> 01:04:45.700
So I finally picked up the phone.

01:04:45.700 --> 01:04:46.920
It was John Podesta,

01:04:46.920 --> 01:04:50.163
the Chief of Staff for
President Clinton calling.

01:04:51.010 --> 01:04:52.137
He says, "Norm," he says.

01:04:52.137 --> 01:04:53.634
"Bill Daley's leaving Commerce.

01:04:53.634 --> 01:04:57.127
"The boss has given me
a short list of names

01:04:57.127 --> 01:05:00.727
"to find out if these
people would be interested

01:05:00.727 --> 01:05:03.747
"in becoming Secretary of Commerce.

01:05:03.747 --> 01:05:05.560
"Would you be interested?"

01:05:05.560 --> 01:05:07.223
And I said, "John,

01:05:07.223 --> 01:05:10.687
"you want me to crawl through
the telephone wire right now

01:05:10.687 --> 01:05:12.297
"to your office,
(audience laughing)

01:05:12.297 --> 01:05:14.360
"to give you my answer?"

01:05:14.360 --> 01:05:17.872
Oh, he said, "Okay, okay,
I'll put you down for yes."

01:05:17.872 --> 01:05:18.820
(audience laughing)

01:05:18.820 --> 01:05:20.840
And then on Thursday morning,

01:05:20.840 --> 01:05:22.700
President Clinton called up,

01:05:22.700 --> 01:05:24.357
he says, "Norm, I'm sending your name

01:05:24.357 --> 01:05:27.398
"up to the Senate this morning,

01:05:27.398 --> 01:05:31.340
"nominating you for
Secretary of Commerce."

01:05:31.340 --> 01:05:34.499
So those were the two interviews,

01:05:34.499 --> 01:05:38.960
two phone interviews I had (coughing)

01:05:38.960 --> 01:05:40.260
for Secretary of Commerce.

01:05:45.280 --> 01:05:47.937
Within about a week to 10 days (coughing),

01:05:53.620 --> 01:05:55.573
I had been confirmed by the Senate.

01:05:57.480 --> 01:06:02.480
Now, they had a Cabinet meeting in June,

01:06:03.660 --> 01:06:06.603
but the one before that was March.

01:06:07.770 --> 01:06:11.143
And so, and then we had one in July.

01:06:12.050 --> 01:06:14.210
And it was supposed to start at 10.

01:06:14.210 --> 01:06:18.488
President didn't come
in until about 10:45,

01:06:18.488 --> 01:06:20.873
and it went until about 12:30.

01:06:22.272 --> 01:06:24.680
With President Bush, you
knew it was gonna start

01:06:24.680 --> 01:06:26.170
at a certain time,

01:06:26.170 --> 01:06:28.050
and it would last one hour.

01:06:28.050 --> 01:06:32.270
We had only two meetings
that went beyond one hour.

01:06:34.550 --> 01:06:39.550
On the 13th of September after 9/11,

01:06:39.640 --> 01:06:42.027
and then in April of '05,

01:06:48.080 --> 01:06:52.440
when they were talking about
the ramp up in the Middle East.

01:06:52.440 --> 01:06:56.050
And so, that meeting went over an hour.

01:06:56.050 --> 01:07:01.050
And there was a televised,
closed circuit program

01:07:01.620 --> 01:07:06.610
with our US Ambassador in Afghanistan,

01:07:06.610 --> 01:07:11.610
and with the commanding general,
I can't recall his name,

01:07:12.200 --> 01:07:13.883
who was in Qatar.

01:07:19.030 --> 01:07:22.800
So we had a three-way Cabinet meeting,

01:07:22.800 --> 01:07:25.413
Cabinet room, Kabul, and Qatar.

01:07:32.110 --> 01:07:34.000
And that one went over an hour.

01:07:34.000 --> 01:07:37.480
The only time we had meeting over an hour.

01:07:37.480 --> 01:07:39.800
But in the case of President Clinton,

01:07:39.800 --> 01:07:42.419
it would be a freewheeling conversation,

01:07:42.419 --> 01:07:44.230
and it could go on for
(Mary laughing)

01:07:44.230 --> 01:07:46.055
an hour and a half.

01:07:46.055 --> 01:07:51.055
And so, but there were just
two different approaches,

01:07:54.643 --> 01:07:58.160
and I really felt that the Cabinet meeting

01:07:58.160 --> 01:08:00.000
under President Bush,

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:03.823
was a vehicle for him to
have a press conference.

01:08:04.962 --> 01:08:08.240
'Cause we would not...

01:08:08.240 --> 01:08:10.880
If you were the Secretary of Defense

01:08:10.880 --> 01:08:12.840
and you brought something up,

01:08:12.840 --> 01:08:16.090
all of us around the table
would not offer suggestions

01:08:16.090 --> 01:08:18.700
or advice or anything.

01:08:18.700 --> 01:08:23.700
And so, it was a vastly
different approach.

01:08:27.530 --> 01:08:29.175
- [Mary] I do have a few questions

01:08:29.175 --> 01:08:31.410
as follow up to your stories

01:08:31.410 --> 01:08:34.509
about the internment camp experience,

01:08:34.509 --> 01:08:37.349
so I'm gonna ask a few of these.

01:08:37.349 --> 01:08:38.370
First one is,

01:08:38.370 --> 01:08:41.300
how has your experience as
an interned Japanese-American

01:08:41.300 --> 01:08:44.293
informed your political
views and career in politics?

01:08:54.400 --> 01:08:59.400
- It has, in terms of my own
service as a city councilman,

01:09:02.880 --> 01:09:05.380
as Mayor, as a member of Congress,

01:09:05.380 --> 01:09:08.550
one of the things I've
always kept in sight,

01:09:13.070 --> 01:09:18.070
was to be able to speak for
those who are not represented,

01:09:18.760 --> 01:09:20.733
or who are underrepresented.

01:09:21.700 --> 01:09:26.700
And so, I always felt
that there were groups

01:09:27.310 --> 01:09:30.450
that were always not represented

01:09:30.450 --> 01:09:33.063
in the actions of the government.

01:09:35.249 --> 01:09:38.930
And so when the whole idea of the ADA,

01:09:45.100 --> 01:09:49.833
the Americans with
Disabilities Act came up,

01:09:51.150 --> 01:09:55.020
with the National Council on Disabilities,

01:09:55.020 --> 01:09:59.583
I wrote the whole transportation
portion of the ADA.

01:10:01.710 --> 01:10:06.710
But, having been interned,
doesn't become the driving wedge,

01:10:09.560 --> 01:10:14.560
but is the backstop that keeps pushing me

01:10:14.660 --> 01:10:18.693
in terms of making sure that people's,

01:10:19.710 --> 01:10:23.520
that their issues, are not disregarded,

01:10:23.520 --> 01:10:25.393
or not dealt with.

01:10:26.300 --> 01:10:28.833
And so, when I became Mayor,

01:10:30.460 --> 01:10:34.480
just as I had been appointed
to the city council in 1967,

01:10:37.680 --> 01:10:41.580
the Asian-American population
in San Jose at that time

01:10:41.580 --> 01:10:43.353
was probably 2.5, 3%.

01:10:47.048 --> 01:10:50.200
The Hispanic population was about 18%.

01:10:50.200 --> 01:10:51.635
So when I became Mayor,

01:10:51.635 --> 01:10:55.520
I left a vacancy on the city council.

01:10:55.520 --> 01:10:58.640
So I said to the Hispanic community,

01:10:58.640 --> 01:11:02.327
"Get together and come up with some names

01:11:02.327 --> 01:11:03.887
"for me to consider,

01:11:03.887 --> 01:11:06.420
"to be appointed to the city council."

01:11:06.420 --> 01:11:10.810
And so I was able to appoint
the first Mexican-American

01:11:10.810 --> 01:11:14.233
to the city council in San Jose.

01:11:15.420 --> 01:11:16.833
But I've always tried to,

01:11:17.710 --> 01:11:22.710
not use the evacuation and
internment as the driving wedge,

01:11:23.120 --> 01:11:27.793
but as a backstop to really
make sure I did my job.

01:11:33.080 --> 01:11:34.680
- [Mary] Another question was,

01:11:34.680 --> 01:11:37.040
what was the attitude of
your friends in the camp

01:11:37.040 --> 01:11:40.060
towards serving in the US Military?

01:11:40.060 --> 01:11:41.380
And I think there's another question

01:11:41.380 --> 01:11:46.380
that another audience member
asked which is quite similar.

01:11:46.580 --> 01:11:48.642
Just, in particular,

01:11:48.642 --> 01:11:51.880
asking about the responses
of your older brother,

01:11:51.880 --> 01:11:56.240
to the loyalty questionnaire
that was issued

01:11:56.240 --> 01:11:57.371
in camp years.

01:11:57.371 --> 01:11:58.204
- Okay.

01:11:58.204 --> 01:12:01.050
The loyalty questionnaire was something

01:12:01.050 --> 01:12:05.543
that was given to all the draft-age males,

01:12:07.537 --> 01:12:09.997
and the (throat clearing),

01:12:13.455 --> 01:12:14.710
there were a number of questions,

01:12:14.710 --> 01:12:19.007
and it came down to, I think
it was question #27 was,

01:12:21.351 --> 01:12:25.910
would you forswear allegiance
to the Emperor of Japan,

01:12:25.910 --> 01:12:29.363
and swear allegiance to the
United States of America.

01:12:30.552 --> 01:12:31.385
And there were a lot of people who said,

01:12:31.385 --> 01:12:34.237
"I've never sworn allegiance
to the Emperor of Japan,

01:12:34.237 --> 01:12:36.547
"so why should I have to sign a document

01:12:36.547 --> 01:12:40.151
"that says I'm gonna
forswear my allegiance

01:12:40.151 --> 01:12:42.980
"to the Emperor of Japan."

01:12:42.980 --> 01:12:46.653
And then question 28 was,

01:12:47.910 --> 01:12:52.910
would you serve in the
military forces, armed forces,

01:12:53.920 --> 01:12:54.993
of the United States?

01:12:56.730 --> 01:12:57.983
And a lot of them says,

01:13:00.290 --> 01:13:04.119
having evacuated and interned
me from my own home State,

01:13:04.119 --> 01:13:06.290
no, I'm not gonna serve.

01:13:06.290 --> 01:13:11.290
So many people answered
no on 27 and no on 28,

01:13:13.356 --> 01:13:16.370
and they became known as the no-no boys.

01:13:16.370 --> 01:13:19.710
And there were, I believe,

01:13:19.710 --> 01:13:24.710
something like 63 from
Heart Mountain, Wyoming,

01:13:25.450 --> 01:13:30.450
who were convicted of
violating federal law,

01:13:32.250 --> 01:13:37.250
and served, maybe, at
least, I'd be guessing,

01:13:38.515 --> 01:13:40.260
about some two to three years

01:13:40.260 --> 01:13:43.220
at McNeil Island in Washington,

01:13:43.220 --> 01:13:44.850
the State of Washington.

01:13:44.850 --> 01:13:48.550
And so there was that
loyalty questionnaire

01:13:48.550 --> 01:13:53.550
that was circulated to
the people, the males,

01:13:56.395 --> 01:14:01.395
and that created a stir
within the community,

01:14:02.160 --> 01:14:07.160
because of the 4C draft classification.

01:14:11.360 --> 01:14:16.193
And they lifted that in the Spring of,

01:14:17.330 --> 01:14:19.663
or the latter part, of 1943.

01:14:22.210 --> 01:14:24.750
And as a result of that,

01:14:24.750 --> 01:14:29.750
the full 42nd regimented
combat team was formed,

01:14:29.780 --> 01:14:33.630
which was an all
Japanese-American military unit

01:14:33.630 --> 01:14:34.963
serving in Europe.

01:14:36.603 --> 01:14:38.060
And when they asked for volunteers,

01:14:38.060 --> 01:14:41.473
and I believe they were
looking for some 4,000,

01:14:42.930 --> 01:14:46.680
they had something like 7,000 volunteers

01:14:46.680 --> 01:14:49.500
from the camps to the Army.

01:14:49.500 --> 01:14:51.110
And I used to always wonder whether or not

01:14:51.110 --> 01:14:54.090
the food in the camp was that much worse

01:14:54.090 --> 01:14:56.800
than what they might
experience in the Army.

01:14:56.800 --> 01:14:58.700
But in any event,

01:14:58.700 --> 01:15:03.700
and the full 42nd ended up, and still is,

01:15:03.896 --> 01:15:08.896
the most highly decorated unit
in US Army Military history.

01:15:10.530 --> 01:15:14.160
And for a regimental combat team,

01:15:14.160 --> 01:15:19.160
which is roughly 4,000
people, they had, I believe,

01:15:19.870 --> 01:15:24.870
23 medals of honor that were
awarded during World War II

01:15:27.898 --> 01:15:29.490
to them.

01:15:29.490 --> 01:15:33.200
So, in fact, President Truman,

01:15:33.200 --> 01:15:38.200
in receiving the full 42nd
back to the United States

01:15:39.090 --> 01:15:40.040
after World War II,

01:15:42.220 --> 01:15:47.220
had a parade of the full 42nd.

01:15:48.690 --> 01:15:53.637
And as he put on the
Presidential Unit Citation

01:15:53.637 --> 01:15:56.383
on their flag,

01:15:57.810 --> 01:16:02.810
he mentioned that the soldiers
had beaten two enemies:

01:16:07.920 --> 01:16:11.853
one in the field, and the
other, prejudice at home.

01:16:14.190 --> 01:16:18.460
And I credit the full
42nd and their action,

01:16:18.460 --> 01:16:20.460
that enabled a lot of us

01:16:20.460 --> 01:16:25.390
to be able to pursue public service,

01:16:25.390 --> 01:16:29.163
because of their record in the military.

01:16:33.470 --> 01:16:36.053
- [Mary] So another follow
up question to that is,

01:16:36.980 --> 01:16:40.620
did you harbor resentment
toward the US government at all

01:16:40.620 --> 01:16:43.010
for interning you and your family?

01:16:43.010 --> 01:16:45.390
If so, how did you overcome that

01:16:45.390 --> 01:16:47.900
to eventually serve at the
level of public service

01:16:47.900 --> 01:16:48.733
that you did?

01:16:51.663 --> 01:16:55.180
- Well, I think the amazing
thing about (throat clearing)

01:16:56.490 --> 01:16:58.003
about this whole story,

01:16:59.560 --> 01:17:04.313
is that 120,000 people went
through this experience,

01:17:05.330 --> 01:17:08.480
and didn't come out with
rancor or bitterness.

01:17:08.480 --> 01:17:13.080
Sure, there are some who
harbored rancor and bitterness.

01:17:13.080 --> 01:17:16.433
But I think the more clary and calm,

01:17:21.962 --> 01:17:25.137
was that most people said,

01:17:25.137 --> 01:17:29.320
"This should never, ever
happen to anyone else again."

01:17:29.320 --> 01:17:31.292
And I think by and large,

01:17:31.292 --> 01:17:34.020
the Japanese-American community

01:17:34.020 --> 01:17:36.290
has been very, very strong

01:17:36.290 --> 01:17:39.940
about making sure that something like this

01:17:39.940 --> 01:17:41.653
never ever happens again.

01:17:42.641 --> 01:17:47.641
I never (throat clearing)
went through any kind of

01:17:47.720 --> 01:17:52.370
feeling of bitterness or
remorse about what happened,

01:17:52.370 --> 01:17:57.370
and I don't think most people

01:17:57.720 --> 01:18:02.720
who had experienced the
internment and evacuation did so,

01:18:04.260 --> 01:18:07.470
but they came out with
a very strong feeling

01:18:07.470 --> 01:18:10.993
that something like this should
never, ever happen again.

01:18:11.840 --> 01:18:16.763
And that's why I worked on,
with a lot of people, the HR442,

01:18:18.370 --> 01:18:22.320
which was the Civil Liberties Act of 1982,

01:18:22.320 --> 01:18:26.160
that was signed into
law by President Reagan.

01:18:26.160 --> 01:18:31.160
And this called for an
apology by the Congress

01:18:32.120 --> 01:18:35.160
to the Japanese-American population,

01:18:35.160 --> 01:18:40.160
and a redress amount that was awarded

01:18:40.610 --> 01:18:43.920
to those that experience redress,

01:18:43.920 --> 01:18:46.453
or, evacuation and internment.

01:18:50.910 --> 01:18:53.743
(throat clearing)

01:18:54.597 --> 01:18:55.980
- [Mary] Along those lines,

01:18:55.980 --> 01:18:58.763
in terms of the current-day
political situation,

01:19:01.500 --> 01:19:03.513
as we've seen in the media today,

01:19:04.350 --> 01:19:05.610
there're constant references

01:19:05.610 --> 01:19:08.020
towards Japanese-American internment

01:19:08.020 --> 01:19:11.300
as a way of dealing with
the current refugee crisis.

01:19:11.300 --> 01:19:14.713
What can you say more about
your response to that?

01:19:17.990 --> 01:19:18.873
- First of all,

01:19:22.610 --> 01:19:26.750
there should be a very strong response

01:19:26.750 --> 01:19:31.750
to anyone who tries to use
an Executive Order 9066,

01:19:31.840 --> 01:19:35.303
or the experience of the
internment of Japanese-Americans

01:19:35.303 --> 01:19:37.563
to apply to anyone today.

01:19:38.523 --> 01:19:43.440
To me, I just don't understand
how someone could be calling

01:19:43.440 --> 01:19:48.327
for the deportation of
all illegal immigrants

01:19:50.530 --> 01:19:52.200
and their families,

01:19:52.200 --> 01:19:54.683
when their families are American citizens.

01:19:55.720 --> 01:19:59.113
I mean, the children of these
people who have immigrated,

01:20:00.100 --> 01:20:05.100
I don't know how they just
summarily deport them.

01:20:06.010 --> 01:20:07.850
They're US citizens,

01:20:07.850 --> 01:20:11.063
and that ought not to
be part of the picture.

01:20:13.130 --> 01:20:18.130
And so, now, I feel very strongly

01:20:18.600 --> 01:20:20.870
that people should not be using

01:20:20.870 --> 01:20:22.920
the evacuation and internment

01:20:22.920 --> 01:20:27.920
as a way of taking care
of the illegals right now.

01:20:30.540 --> 01:20:33.960
They've already interned
women and children

01:20:33.960 --> 01:20:35.603
who have come across the border,

01:20:38.148 --> 01:20:39.840
and again, I just don't think

01:20:40.861 --> 01:20:42.723
that's the way to solve the problem.

01:20:44.190 --> 01:20:47.357
(audience applauding)

01:20:53.070 --> 01:20:54.540
- [Mary] So I think we have
time for one last question,

01:20:54.540 --> 01:20:56.000
and this is an appropriate one.

01:20:56.000 --> 01:20:58.050
I think I actually got through the stack!

01:20:59.610 --> 01:21:00.890
So in your experience,

01:21:00.890 --> 01:21:03.680
what are the three most
essential traits of leadership,

01:21:03.680 --> 01:21:04.573
being a leader?

01:21:21.317 --> 01:21:26.317
- Well, the first one is something that,

01:21:26.853 --> 01:21:28.803
there're two things that all of us own,

01:21:30.090 --> 01:21:32.633
and we should not give that up in any way.

01:21:33.550 --> 01:21:34.803
One is our name.

01:21:35.720 --> 01:21:37.393
And the second is our integrity.

01:21:38.490 --> 01:21:41.143
And no matter what the
circumstance might be,

01:21:42.518 --> 01:21:47.518
we ought not to compromise
our own principles and ideals

01:21:48.320 --> 01:21:51.810
just for some short term gain,
(scattered applauding)

01:21:51.810 --> 01:21:53.620
or even long term gain.

01:21:53.620 --> 01:21:57.693
To me, the whole name of
the game is integrity,

01:22:00.087 --> 01:22:05.087
and it just makes me sick
when I hear about people

01:22:06.180 --> 01:22:10.780
who couch their answers

01:22:12.750 --> 01:22:15.493
to whatever the question might be.

01:22:19.871 --> 01:22:21.790
But the other thing that just, to me,

01:22:23.560 --> 01:22:24.573
there is so much,

01:22:27.420 --> 01:22:31.681
well, I don't know necessarily corruption,

01:22:31.681 --> 01:22:32.981
but there is just too much

01:22:35.840 --> 01:22:39.853
people not standing by
their own principles.

01:22:41.490 --> 01:22:44.090
And so, in a way,

01:22:44.090 --> 01:22:48.110
I'd say one is integrity,
two is integrity,

01:22:48.110 --> 01:22:49.266
three is integrity.

01:22:49.266 --> 01:22:51.512
(audience laughing)

01:22:51.512 --> 01:22:56.512
But there are leadership
traits that people,

01:22:57.329 --> 01:22:58.410
I think, should have.

01:22:58.410 --> 01:23:01.927
And today, when I hear people saying,

01:23:01.927 --> 01:23:04.167
"Well, I'm not a politician."

01:23:05.485 --> 01:23:07.586
Well then why are you
running for public office?

01:23:07.586 --> 01:23:10.586
(audience laughing)

01:23:12.440 --> 01:23:14.857
You wouldn't go to a
brain surgeon who says,

01:23:14.857 --> 01:23:16.647
"I'm really not a brain surgeon,

01:23:16.647 --> 01:23:19.163
"I just practice every day at it."

01:23:19.163 --> 01:23:21.330
(audience laughing)

01:23:21.330 --> 01:23:23.923
And to me,

01:23:24.760 --> 01:23:28.540
regardless of what profession
or background we are in,

01:23:28.540 --> 01:23:31.220
we wanna deal with subject matter experts.

01:23:31.220 --> 01:23:33.550
People who know what
they're talking about.

01:23:33.550 --> 01:23:37.333
People who devote their time to the study

01:23:37.333 --> 01:23:39.393
of what it is they're doing.

01:23:40.350 --> 01:23:45.350
And I just don't see that
in a lot of instances today.

01:23:47.381 --> 01:23:50.693
So, in any event,

01:23:52.806 --> 01:23:57.806
the integrity's still, no
matter how I look at it,

01:23:58.240 --> 01:23:59.337
is still number one.

01:24:02.860 --> 01:24:06.027
(audience applauding)

01:24:12.960 --> 01:24:14.793
Thank you.
- Thank you.

01:24:18.752 --> 01:24:20.655
Wonderful, thank you.

01:24:20.655 --> 01:24:21.543
- Thank you very much everybody.

01:24:31.880 --> 01:24:34.680
- Thank you all so much for coming, and,

01:24:34.680 --> 01:24:35.980
well, this was wonderful.

01:24:35.980 --> 01:24:38.530
And I can't thank you
enough for taking time out

01:24:38.530 --> 01:24:39.470
to come see us.

01:24:39.470 --> 01:24:41.740
You have no idea how busy his schedule is,

01:24:41.740 --> 01:24:45.005
even though he's retired
from public service.

01:24:45.005 --> 01:24:47.760
(audience laughing)

01:24:47.760 --> 01:24:49.353
- Thank you very much everyone.

01:24:50.544 --> 01:24:51.377
- Okay.

